#: package/analyses/jjdotplotstats.description
msgid ""
"Compares a continuous variable across groups and draws the comparison\n"
"horizontally - values on the x axis, group labels down the y axis - with\n"
"an optional vertical reference line. Wraps ggstatsplot::ggbetweenstats\n"
"and ggstatsplot::grouped_ggbetweenstats, so the figure is a box-violin\n"
"plot with the individual observations shown, and the test is a\n"
"between-groups comparison using every observation."
msgstr ""

#: jjdotplotstats/options.description.main
msgid ""
"Compares a continuous variable across groups and draws the comparison\n"
"horizontally - values on the x axis, group labels down the y axis - with\n"
"an optional vertical reference line. Wraps ggstatsplot::ggbetweenstats\n"
"and ggstatsplot::grouped_ggbetweenstats, so the figure is a box-violin\n"
"plot with the individual observations shown, and the test is a\n"
"between-groups comparison using every observation.\n"
"\n"
"This analysis was previously titled \"Dot Chart\", which described neither\n"
"the figure nor the statistic: it draws violins and boxplots, not a dot\n"
"chart, and it is a between-groups test rather than a one-sample one. For\n"
"a genuine Cleveland dot chart - one summary point per label, tested\n"
"against a reference value - use \"Dot Chart (Summary vs Reference Value)\",\n"
"which wraps ggstatsplot::ggdotplotstats.\n"
"\n"
"Prefer this over \"Box-Violin Plots to Compare Between Groups\" when the\n"
"group labels are long or numerous, since the horizontal layout gives them\n"
"room, or when a clinical threshold line is useful."
msgstr ""

#: R/benford.b.R
msgid ""
"Compares the distribution of leading digits in your data against Benford's "
"Law, which describes how leading digits are distributed in data spanning "
"several orders of magnitude. A departure indicates the values do not follow "
"that pattern; it does not identify why."
msgstr ""

#: package/analyses/benford.description
#: benford/options.description.main
msgid ""
"Compares the leading digits of a positive numeric variable against Benford's "
"Law, using a chi-square goodness-of-fit test, the Mantissa Arc Test and "
"Nigrini's MAD conformity classification. The MAD classification is reported "
"only when the sample is large enough for its cut-offs to exceed sampling "
"noise; otherwise the chi-square test carries the finding. Benford's Law "
"applies to data spanning at least two orders of magnitude. The analysis "
"reports whether a departure from the expected leading-digit frequencies was "
"detected; it does not identify a cause."
msgstr ""

#: package/analyses/door.description
#: door/options.description.main
msgid ""
"Compares two groups on an ordinal Desirability of Outcome Ranking (DOOR), a "
"composite that ranks each patient by overall clinical desirability combining "
"efficacy and safety. The DOOR probability is the chance that a randomly "
"chosen patient from the index group has a more desirable outcome than a "
"randomly chosen patient from the reference group (ties split evenly) - "
"equivalent to a Mann-Whitney / AUC estimand. Values above 0.5 favour the "
"index group. This approach, developed for antimicrobial and benefit-risk "
"trials, avoids collapsing outcomes of differing severity into a single "
"binary endpoint."
msgstr ""

