#: singlearm/options/uselandmark.description
msgid ""
"Performs a conditional landmark description: only subjects still event-free "
"and under observation after the landmark are retained, and their time scale "
"is reset there. This changes the target population and does not by itself "
"remove immortal-time bias, estimate a treatment effect, or justify excluding "
"early events."
msgstr ""

#: package/analyses/bayesianmetaanalysis.description
msgid ""
"Performs Bayesian meta-analysis using hierarchical models for evidence \n"
"synthesis across multiple studies. This approach accounts for both \n"
"within-study and between-study variability using informative prior \n"
"distributions and MCMC sampling methods."
msgstr ""

#: bayesianmetaanalysis/options.description.main
msgid ""
"Performs Bayesian meta-analysis using hierarchical models for evidence \n"
"synthesis across multiple studies. This approach accounts for both \n"
"within-study and between-study variability using informative prior \n"
"distributions and MCMC sampling methods.\n"
"\n"
"Key features:\n"
"- Random effects and fixed effects Bayesian meta-analysis\n"
"- Multiple outcome types (continuous, binary, time-to-event)\n"
"- Hierarchical modeling with informative and non-informative priors\n"
"- MCMC sampling with convergence diagnostics\n"
"- Posterior predictive checking and model comparison\n"
"- Meta-regression with study-level covariates\n"
"- Publication bias assessment and adjustment"
msgstr ""

#: package/analyses/chisqposttest.description
#: chisqposttest/options.description.main
msgid ""
"Performs Chi-Square test and post-hoc pairwise comparisons. Post-hoc "
"pairwise comparisons are ONLY performed when the overall chi-square test is "
"significant (p < α). This enforces proper statistical workflow and prevents "
"data dredging. Selecting 'None' for post-hoc method DISABLES all pairwise "
"testing. If you want unadjusted pairwise comparisons, this feature is not "
"available (by design, as it would encourage inappropriate multiple testing). "
"No automated validation against established packages exists. Use with "
"caution for clinical decision-making."
msgstr ""

#: package/analyses/simonmakuch.description
#: simonmakuch/options.description.main
msgid ""
"Performs comprehensive survival analysis with time-dependent variables using "
"the Simon-Makuch method. This specialized analysis addresses the critical "
"challenge of immortal time bias when studying treatments, biomarkers, or "
"exposures that change during patient follow-up.\n"
"**Clinical Applications:** - Treatment effectiveness when therapy timing "
"varies (e.g., adjuvant therapy, targeted agents) - Biomarker evolution "
"studies (e.g., mutation status, protein expression changes) - Disease "
"progression impact on outcomes (e.g., metastasis development, grade "
"progression) - Healthcare intervention timing effects (e.g., surgery, "
"radiation, supportive care)\n"
"**Key Statistical Methods:** - Simon-Makuch plots: Modified Kaplan-Meier "
"curves for time-dependent exposures - Landmark analysis: Conditional "
"survival from fixed time points - Time-dependent Cox regression: Hazard "
"ratios accounting for exposure timing - Immortal time bias assessment: "
"Comparison of naive vs. proper methods - Mantel-Byar testing: Specialized "
"tests for time-dependent comparisons\n"
"**Why Use Simon-Makuch Analysis:** Standard survival analysis assumes fixed "
"baseline characteristics, leading to immortal time bias when exposures "
"change during follow-up. Simon-Makuch methods properly account for the "
"timing of exposure changes, providing unbiased survival estimates and valid "
"statistical comparisons."
msgstr ""

#: package/analyses/conditionalsurvival.description
#: conditionalsurvival/options.description.main
msgid ""
"Performs conditional survival estimation, which calculates the probability  "
"of surviving beyond a specific time point given survival to a conditioning  "
"time point. This analysis is particularly useful for updating prognosis  "
"estimates for patients who have already survived a certain period. Multiple  "
"estimation methods are supported including Kaplan-Meier weights, landmark  "
"approaches, and inverse probability weighting."
msgstr ""

#: package/analyses/mixedcox.description
#: mixedcox/options.description.main
msgid ""
"Performs Cox proportional hazards regression with random effects for  "
"clustered or hierarchical survival data. This method accounts for  "
"correlation within clusters (e.g., patients within hospitals, multiple  "
"events per patient) using mixed-effects modeling. The random effects  "
"capture cluster-specific variation while estimating population-level  fixed "
"effects."
msgstr ""

#: package/analyses/timevarycox.description
#: timevarycox/options.description.main
msgid ""
"Performs Cox proportional hazards regression with time-varying covariates.  "
"This analysis allows covariates to change values over time during follow-up, "
" which is essential for modeling dynamic clinical variables such as "
"treatment  changes, biomarker levels, or disease progression markers."
msgstr ""

#: package/analyses/directbinomial.description
#: directbinomial/options.description.main
msgid ""
"Performs direct binomial regression modeling for cumulative incidence  "
"functions in competing risks analysis using the timereg package. This  "
"method provides direct modeling of cumulative incidence without  "
"proportional subdistribution hazards assumptions."
msgstr ""

#: package/analyses/flexcomprisk.description
#: flexcomprisk/options.description.main
msgid ""
"Performs flexible competing risks analysis using advanced regression  "
"techniques including spline-based hazards, time-varying effects, and  robust "
"prediction models with comprehensive model validation."
msgstr ""

#: package/analyses/highdimcox.description
#: highdimcox/options.description.main
msgid ""
"Performs high-dimensional Cox proportional hazards regression using advanced "
"regularization methods (LASSO, Ridge, Elastic Net, Adaptive LASSO) for  "
"survival data with many predictors. This method is essential when the number "
"of variables is large relative to the number of observations, enabling "
"variable selection and preventing overfitting in genomic, proteomic, or "
"other high-dimensional clinical datasets."
msgstr ""

#: package/analyses/lassologistic.description
#: lassologistic/options.description.main
msgid ""
"Performs LASSO-penalized logistic regression for variable selection in "
"binary\n"
"classification problems. Ideal for diagnostic pathology studies that build\n"
"classifiers (e.g., tumor type A vs B) with automatic feature selection."
msgstr ""

#: package/analyses/leaveonecenterout.description
#: leaveonecenterout/options.description.main
msgid ""
"Performs leave-one-center-out cross-validation for multi-institutional\n"
"prediction models. Trains on all-but-one center and evaluates on the\n"
"held-out center, repeating for each center. Provides internal-external\n"
"validation recommended by TRIPOD and Debray et al. (2015)."
msgstr ""

#: package/analyses/multisurvival.description
#: multisurvival/options.description.main
msgid ""
"Performs multivariable survival analysis using Cox proportional hazards "
"regression. In multivariable survival analysis, person-time follow-up is "
"crucial for properly adjusting for covariates while accounting for varying "
"observation periods. The Cox proportional hazards model incorporates "
"person-time by modeling the hazard function, which represents the "
"instantaneous event rate per unit of person-time. When stratifying analyses "
"or examining multiple predictors, the model accounts for how these factors "
"influence event rates relative to the person-time at risk in each subgroup."
msgstr ""

#: package/analyses/pcaloadingtest.description
msgid ""
"Performs permutation-based significance testing for PCA loadings using the\n"
"permV method (Linting et al., 2011). This approach permutes one variable at\n"
"a time, providing variable-specific and component-specific significance "
"thresholds\n"
"with higher statistical power and proper Type I error control."
msgstr ""

#: pcaloadingtest/options.description.main
msgid ""
"Performs permutation-based significance testing for PCA loadings using the\n"
"permV method (Linting et al., 2011). This approach permutes one variable at\n"
"a time, providing variable-specific and component-specific significance "
"thresholds\n"
"with higher statistical power and proper Type I error control.\n"
"\n"
"Instead of arbitrary cutoffs (e.g., |loading| > 0.5), this test provides\n"
"objective, hypothesis-tested thresholds for each variable on each component.\n"
"Procrustes rotation is applied to handle sign reflection and component\n"
"indeterminacy issues."
msgstr ""

#: package/analyses/permutationsurvival.description
#: permutationsurvival/options.description.main
msgid ""
"Performs permutation-based tests for comparing survival distributions "
"between groups. These tests provide non-parametric alternatives to "
"traditional log-rank tests that do not rely on asymptotic theory, making "
"them particularly suitable for small samples or when distributional "
"assumptions are violated. Includes various permutation strategies and "
"multiple comparison corrections for robust survival analysis."
msgstr ""

#: package/analyses/rmst.description
#: rmst/options.description.main
msgid ""
"Performs restricted mean survival time (RMST) analysis for comparing  "
"survival distributions between groups. RMST provides a clinically meaningful "
" measure of survival benefit by estimating the area under the survival curve "
" up to a specific time point (tau), representing the average survival time  "
"within the restriction period."
msgstr ""

#: package/analyses/pcacomponenttest.description
msgid ""
"Performs SEQUENTIAL permutation-based significance testing to determine "
"which principal\n"
"components explain more variance than expected by random chance. This "
"provides\n"
"an objective, hypothesis-tested approach to component retention."
msgstr ""

#: pcacomponenttest/options.description.main
msgid ""
"Performs SEQUENTIAL permutation-based significance testing to determine "
"which principal\n"
"components explain more variance than expected by random chance. This "
"provides\n"
"an objective, hypothesis-tested approach to component retention.\n"
"\n"
"The test uses the Buja & Eyuboglu (1992) sequential method where:\n"
"1. Each component is tested against a permutation null distribution\n"
"2. Significant components have their variance REMOVED before testing the "
"next component\n"
"3. Testing STOPS when the first non-significant component is found\n"
"\n"
"This sequential approach prevents inflated Type I errors that occur when all "
"components\n"
"are tested against the same null distribution (batch testing).\n"
"\n"
"CRITICAL: Requires centered and scaled data for valid correlation-based "
"interpretation.\n"
"Without centering/scaling, the test compares raw variance instead of "
"correlation structure."
msgstr ""

#: package/analyses/mediansurvival.description
#: mediansurvival/options.description.main
msgid ""
"Performs statistical comparisons of median survival times between groups. "
"Provides confidence intervals, hypothesis tests, and robust estimators for  "
"median survival with handling of censored data and group comparisons."
msgstr ""

#: package/analyses/singlearm.description
#: singlearm/options.description.main
msgid ""
"Performs survival analysis for a single cohort without group comparisons. "
"Kaplan-Meier estimates use event times and risk sets; in competing-risk "
"mode, cumulative incidence retains competing terminal events as separate "
"states. Optional person-time rates use the sum of individual observation "
"periods as their denominator. This is descriptive analysis of one cohort, "
"not a treatment-effect estimate."
msgstr ""

#: package/analyses/timeupdatedsurvival.description
#: timeupdatedsurvival/options.description.main
msgid ""
"Performs time-updated survival estimates using dynamic regression models  "
"for survival data. This analysis allows for time-varying effects of "
"covariates,  dynamic hazard functions, and temporal changes in survival "
"relationships.  Multiple modeling approaches are supported including Aalen "
"additive models,  Cox-Aalen hybrid models, and dynamic regression with "
"time-updated coefficients."
msgstr ""

#: package/analyses/survivalfeaturerank.description
#: survivalfeaturerank/options.description.main
msgid ""
"Performs univariate survival analysis for multiple features to identify "
"potential prognostic factors. This analysis runs a separate Cox proportional "
"hazards model for each selected feature and ranks them by statistical "
"significance, hazard ratio, or concordance index. Useful for biomarker "
"screening, exploratory analysis, and feature selection before building "
"multivariable models."
msgstr ""

#: package/analyses/survival.description
#: survival/options.description.main
msgid ""
"Performs univariate time-to-event analysis comparing groups using "
"Kaplan-Meier estimates, log-rank tests, and Cox proportional hazards "
"regression. Optional outputs include restricted mean survival time and crude "
"person-time incidence rates. These methods describe associations and do not "
"establish causality."
msgstr ""

#: package/analyses/weightedlogrank.description
#: weightedlogrank/options.description.main
msgid ""
"Performs weighted log-rank tests for comparing survival distributions "
"between groups. These tests provide enhanced power for detecting differences "
"at specific time points using various weighting schemes. Includes "
"Gehan-Wilcoxon, Tarone-Ware, Peto-Peto, and custom weight function "
"approaches for comprehensive survival comparison."
msgstr ""

